WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.508 --> 00:00:05.006
Hello everyone! Welcome to the channel "Mathematical Experiments."

00:00:05.006 --> 00:00:07.365
I'm Artem Vorozhtsov.

00:00:07.365 --> 00:00:16.012
And today I want to continue the topic of the formula: the sum of the s-th powers of the first "x" natural numbers.

00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:19.982
The thing is, I forgot to mention one important thing.

00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:22.747
The Euler-Maclaurin formula.

00:00:22.747 --> 00:00:25.266
Well, not just one thing, I forgot to mention many important things.

00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:30.000
In particular, in that video, I forgot to mention the actual answer.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.099
What the Bernoulli polynomials look like when written explicitly, in the form of a formula.

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:42.706
Also, today I want to show you some tricks.

00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:46.384
Today's video has two stars,

00:00:46.384 --> 00:00:51.011
where I will cover elements of advanced math.

00:00:51.011 --> 00:00:59.512
These are tricks that you won't often find in textbooks, but they are mentioned there.

00:00:59.512 --> 00:01:02.165
Inevitably in proper textbooks.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:10.932
You won't find them often in textbooks because they are not related to proving formulas.

00:01:10.932 --> 00:01:15.523
They are related to the process of finding answers.

00:01:15.523 --> 00:01:19.676
And they are, let's say, not entirely fair.

00:01:19.676 --> 00:01:20.653
Sneaky.

00:01:20.653 --> 00:01:24.258
To strictly justify them, you need to write a lot of words,

00:01:24.258 --> 00:01:29.215
but textbooks, you know, don't have that much space.

00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:39.550
And our intermediate goal will be the formula 1/√n. The sum up to x.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:46.200
I will need the Taylor series formula for the exponential function in this video.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.540
Here it is.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.391
I covered it in my previous video.

00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:59.306
About how this series of basic functions (x^n)/n! has this remarkable property

00:01:59.306 --> 00:02:04.667
that their derivatives give the same series.

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:05.459
Alright.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:12.719
So, let's fill in the gap from the previous video and write out the formula.

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:14.059
Here it is.

00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:18.961
The resulting polynomials have the following formula.

00:02:18.961 --> 00:02:31.035
These polynomials are equal to the sum of the s-th powers of the first x natural numbers.

00:02:31.035 --> 00:02:36.000
These polynomials include the first term,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.482
which many recognize as an integral, those who studied calculus,

00:02:40.482 --> 00:02:48.765
and the second part, the sum over k of binomial coefficients,

00:02:48.765 --> 00:02:54.255
multiplied by certain constants Zeta[-k].

00:02:54.255 --> 00:02:55.888
Let's just call them constants.

00:02:55.888 --> 00:03:01.928
Magical constants, numbered by the natural number k.

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:07.214
And these constants appear for various values of s.

00:03:07.214 --> 00:03:12.757
For 1, 2, and 3, the same constants Zeta[-k] appear.

00:03:12.757 --> 00:03:17.531
We simply discovered them, and we still don't understand the values of the zeta function.

00:03:17.531 --> 00:03:19.017
We haven't proven it.

00:03:19.017 --> 00:03:26.348
And by the way, maybe today we will finally gain some intuition,

00:03:26.348 --> 00:03:36.312
that most likely it is the analytic continuation of some function, which I will define today.

00:03:36.312 --> 00:03:38.112
But not a proof, just intuition.

00:03:38.112 --> 00:03:43.032
We don't have proofs on our channel, as you know, as you have probably noticed.

00:03:45.735 --> 00:03:52.253
Yes. In fact, the formula we obtained sums up to s-1.

00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:57.394
But here, we really want to extend this sum up to s.

00:03:57.394 --> 00:04:01.903
It's not so scary to add the last term for k = s,

00:04:01.903 --> 00:04:05.325
because that term is the constant term.

00:04:05.325 --> 00:04:09.113
When s = k, we get x^0.

00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:11.661
Which is just some number.

00:04:11.661 --> 00:04:13.330
Let's see...

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:16.228
Slap!

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:22.718
Here, for example, Hr[x, 4].

00:04:22.718 --> 00:04:32.061
This is what the sum of the fourth powers of the natural numbers from 1 to x looks like. This is the result.

00:04:33.595 --> 00:04:36.000
Let's write down these magical coefficients,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.472
which are hidden in the coefficients of all these Bernoulli polynomials.

00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:45.816
They start with -1/2, -1/12,

00:04:45.816 --> 00:04:50.702
and then there are zeros every other number,

00:04:50.702 --> 00:04:53.031
and strange, mysterious numbers.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.615
The formula for the sum of the s-th powers of the first x natural numbers

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:08.081
in Mathematica is known as HarmonicNumber.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.826
But you see, there is a discrepancy for odd HR[x, 5],

00:05:17.826 --> 00:05:22.127
because I added this constant term that is actually absent.

00:05:22.127 --> 00:05:31.725
Well, indeed, when you sum up the s-th powers of the natural numbers where x = 0, it should be 0.

00:05:31.725 --> 00:05:33.675
But mine is not 0.

00:05:33.675 --> 00:05:35.092
Well, you understand, it's not a big deal.

00:05:35.092 --> 00:05:40.818
The main thing is that this polynomial of mine,

00:05:40.818 --> 00:05:48.916
when x is increased by 1, x^s is added to it.

00:05:52.642 --> 00:05:57.703
The Bernoulli polynomials defined in Mathematica,

00:05:57.703 --> 00:06:03.794
how are they different from the polynomial HR that I defined?

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.544
Stop... Let me input the number 6.

00:06:12.822 --> 00:06:20.271
First, they need to be divided by "s". By what I call "s".

00:06:20.271 --> 00:06:23.770
Second, we need to take a higher power.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.706
I have 5, while Bernoulli has 6.

00:06:27.706 --> 00:06:35.090
The arguments are swapped, and there's also a difference in the sign here.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:36.761
That's not so important.

00:06:36.761 --> 00:06:47.015
Simply put, Bernoulli polynomials sum the natural numbers up to x - 1, not up to x. That's the only difference.

00:06:47.015 --> 00:06:48.475
What's important here?

00:06:48.475 --> 00:06:55.477
The main thing is that the difference between my polynomials for x and x-1 gives x^5,

00:06:55.477 --> 00:06:59.155
while for Bernoulli, the same thing gives (x-1)^5.

00:06:59.155 --> 00:07:05.663
That's the main property of the polynomials we were looking for.

00:07:05.663 --> 00:07:11.194
And of course, for this property, the constant term is simply not important.

00:07:11.194 --> 00:07:12.498
Moving on...

00:07:14.544 --> 00:07:16.916
This formula,

00:07:16.916 --> 00:07:26.147
that the difference of two HR polynomials is simply the sum of the s-th powers of natural numbers from a+1 to b.

00:07:26.147 --> 00:07:30.485
Very reminiscent of the Leibniz formula.

00:07:30.485 --> 00:07:35.515
Reminiscent only to those who have studied the Leibniz formula.

00:07:35.515 --> 00:07:41.464
So... It's a direct analogy, as you understand.

00:07:41.464 --> 00:07:45.786
Except here, it's not integration, but discrete integration.

00:07:45.786 --> 00:07:53.033
Essentially, it's also an area, but the area of columns, not the area under a smooth curve.

00:07:53.033 --> 00:07:58.788
I will show you this visualization with the columns.

00:07:58.788 --> 00:08:09.753
Let's go back a bit now and for those who missed it, let's show how the HR formula was derived.

00:08:09.753 --> 00:08:12.000
It wasn't derived, as always.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.817
It was discovered.

00:08:13.817 --> 00:08:21.772
We don't derive, we don't prove, we just suddenly discover some beautiful formula.

00:08:21.772 --> 00:08:23.434
It all starts innocently.

00:08:23.434 --> 00:08:26.968
We write down Pascal's Triangle in this form.

00:08:26.968 --> 00:08:32.813
We move this leading term x^s to the left.

00:08:32.813 --> 00:08:36.176
Here are the first rows of Pascal's Triangle.

00:08:36.176 --> 00:08:42.453
And let's denote this difference as yₖ[x].

00:08:45.485 --> 00:08:54.254
It's unclear how this idea came to mind,

00:08:54.254 --> 00:08:55.619
but forgive me for that.

00:08:55.619 --> 00:09:02.222
In fact, you can reach this idea independently. I won't focus on that.

00:09:02.222 --> 00:09:03.430
It's not some kind of magic.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.706
When a person thinks about the sum of k-th powers,

00:09:06.706 --> 00:09:12.286
this idea appears in their mind at some point.

00:09:12.286 --> 00:09:19.704
You just have to think, think, think for a few weeks, and this idea will come to your mind.

00:09:19.704 --> 00:09:21.622
It could have come up, but it's too late now.

00:09:21.622 --> 00:09:26.177
Now you already have the answer, and the experiment would be unfair.

00:09:26.177 --> 00:09:29.541
So, the idea arises:

00:09:29.541 --> 00:09:31.950
to write down Pascal's Triangle

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:40.231
and then try to express x^k in terms of y's.

00:09:40.231 --> 00:09:43.442
The first answers are given easily.

00:09:43.442 --> 00:09:45.988
x^0 is y1.

00:09:45.988 --> 00:09:47.111
And x1,

00:09:47.111 --> 00:09:49.148
which is x^1,

00:09:49.148 --> 00:09:52.061
is expressed in terms of y2 and 1,

00:09:52.061 --> 00:09:54.061
and 1 is y1.

00:09:57.897 --> 00:10:01.285
It gets harder from here.

00:10:01.285 --> 00:10:08.141
What do we need to do to obtain the formula for x^2?

00:10:08.141 --> 00:10:14.200
We need to take the third equation,

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:19.230
multiply it by 2, and subtract 3 times the second equation from it.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:23.566
Then these 3x and 2x will cancel out.

00:10:25.795 --> 00:10:33.104
Then we need to determine the constant term and eliminate it using the third term.

00:10:33.104 --> 00:10:35.076
Here's what we get.

00:10:35.076 --> 00:10:37.953
Pause and think.

00:10:37.953 --> 00:10:47.370
Amazingly, this expression is the polynomial HR of x-1.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:48.893
Moving on.

00:10:50.683 --> 00:10:55.673
Now let's take the 4th rows of Pascal's Triangle

00:10:55.673 --> 00:10:59.278
and do the same thing for x^3.

00:10:59.278 --> 00:11:02.189
What do we need to do to obtain x^3?

00:11:02.189 --> 00:11:08.059
We need to subtract 2 times the penultimate equation from the last equation.

00:11:08.059 --> 00:11:11.660
Then these 6x^2 and 3x^2 will cancel out.

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:14.514
Well, then you'll see x's and constant terms.

00:11:14.514 --> 00:11:19.430
The constant terms actually turn out to be zero.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.231
And here, y1 is absent.

00:11:22.231 --> 00:11:23.571
Alright.

00:11:23.571 --> 00:11:29.091
Amazingly, this coincides with HR for s = 3.

00:11:29.091 --> 00:11:36.568
Except for that additional constant term, which is added for the beauty of the final formula.

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:43.717
Well, why did this work? The answer is quite simple.

00:11:43.717 --> 00:11:48.000
Here, for example, we obtained this equality.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.274
And now we sum this equality over x from 0 to n.

00:11:56.685 --> 00:11:58.014
We sum it.

00:11:58.014 --> 00:12:00.672
On the left, we get the desired result...

00:12:02.316 --> 00:12:07.094
More precisely, we obtain the formula for which we want to find the answer.

00:12:07.094 --> 00:12:11.832
The sum of squares of the first x natural numbers.

00:12:11.832 --> 00:12:14.273
And here, such sums are obtained.

00:12:14.273 --> 00:12:22.152
These sums are interesting because they contain terms that cancel out when you sum them.

00:12:22.152 --> 00:12:25.322
Think about this formula

00:12:25.322 --> 00:12:33.094
and understand that when you sum it, only ((1+n)^1)/6 remains.

00:12:34.007 --> 00:12:37.423
Well, the same thing here.

00:12:37.423 --> 00:12:45.741
This is one way to obtain the formula for the sum of squares of the first n natural numbers.

00:12:45.741 --> 00:12:54.890
And for those who are familiar with matrices, everything looks somewhat easier.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:13:01.856
The first n rows of Pascal's Triangle is expressed as a single, but matrix equation.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:07.040
And all we need to do is invert this matrix.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:11.668
We calculate the inverse of this matrix, examine it,

00:13:11.668 --> 00:13:17.285
and with surprise, we discover zeros on these diagonals.

00:13:17.285 --> 00:13:21.782
We call this the discovered pattern.

00:13:26.202 --> 00:13:34.797
If we calculate this matrix, choosing a larger size n,

00:13:34.797 --> 00:13:40.197
we see that not only zeros become visible to us,

00:13:40.197 --> 00:13:45.957
but the non-zero diagonals are also structured in a particular way.

00:13:45.957 --> 00:13:53.617
This number 691, which mathematicians know by heart,

00:13:53.617 --> 00:14:05.663
suggests to us that the numbers on the diagonals consist of one particular number multiplied by something.

00:14:05.663 --> 00:14:10.413
And how can we discover what that something is?

00:14:10.413 --> 00:14:11.498
Well, it's very simple.

00:14:11.498 --> 00:14:15.252
Let's divide the entire diagonal by 1/30.

00:14:15.252 --> 00:14:20.318
Let's divide the entire diagonal by 5/66 and so on...

00:14:20.318 --> 00:14:22.901
These are the things we do.

00:14:22.901 --> 00:14:25.959
Binomial coefficients are discovered there.

00:14:25.959 --> 00:14:36.726
And then after dividing by binomial coefficients, we understand how our matrix is structured.

00:14:36.726 --> 00:14:38.595
Let me skip some steps here.

00:14:38.595 --> 00:14:42.967
If you don't understand, I refer you to my video.

00:14:42.967 --> 00:14:50.202
In short, we see that the coefficients in this inverse matrix consist of the product

00:14:50.202 --> 00:14:52.780
of binomial coefficients

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:58.029
and a number that is written here.

00:14:58.029 --> 00:15:02.142
Each diagonal consists of a number

00:15:02.142 --> 00:15:04.481
which we denote as Zeta[-k],

00:15:04.481 --> 00:15:07.723
and a binomial coefficient.

00:15:07.723 --> 00:15:16.012
And this part simply corresponds to the main diagonal of this matrix.

00:15:17.854 --> 00:15:20.789
Now we have an explicit formula.

00:15:20.789 --> 00:15:27.682
Yes, here we sum up to s-1, but I always add just s everywhere.

00:15:30.604 --> 00:15:33.980
Let's take a look at this formula.

00:15:36.146 --> 00:15:43.311
Binomial coefficient [s, k] multiplied by x^(s-k).

00:15:43.311 --> 00:15:47.530
Oh, how I wish there was something raised to the power of k here.

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:54.993
Then it would be exactly the formula of Newton's binomial.

00:15:54.993 --> 00:16:02.752
Let's allow ourselves to do that and replace Zeta[-k] with z^k.

00:16:02.752 --> 00:16:08.038
Then this expression becomes the following.

00:16:10.814 --> 00:16:17.252
z is not a number. It's just a little hack, a trick.

00:16:17.252 --> 00:16:24.912
Let's allow ourselves to replace the sequence Zeta[-k] with z^k.

00:16:24.912 --> 00:16:29.540
To see the structure of the formula.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:32.707
Of course, such z does not exist.

00:16:32.707 --> 00:16:39.493
It's impossible to imagine such z that would satisfy these equations.

00:16:39.493 --> 00:16:45.584
But nevertheless, let's take a closer look at the result.

00:16:45.584 --> 00:16:49.828
And we'll see something interesting here,

00:16:49.828 --> 00:16:52.118
that this

00:16:52.118 --> 00:16:56.456
is simply the integral of this.

00:16:56.456 --> 00:17:01.308
And let's allow ourselves to generalize this equation

00:17:01.308 --> 00:17:03.308
to this equation.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:13.610
As if here, instead of t^s, you could take any function of t.

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:22.914
This idea naturally arises for those who know that functions can be expanded into Taylor series.

00:17:22.914 --> 00:17:28.094
If you obtained something for t^s,

00:17:28.094 --> 00:17:34.478
then perhaps it is true for any other function that can be expanded into a sum of powers of t.

00:17:38.533 --> 00:17:48.380
But here we need to go back to the fact that z is not just some number, but a tricky thing.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:56.844
Here, we take that very Taylor series and write z^k through Zeta[-k].

00:17:56.844 --> 00:18:07.873
This trick, this cleverness, allowed us to quickly go from Pascal's Triangle and the formula for s-powers

00:18:07.873 --> 00:18:13.344
to the Euler-Maclaurin formula.

00:18:13.344 --> 00:18:17.760
What is written here is an incorrect formula.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:22.023
You have to forgive me for now.

00:18:22.863 --> 00:18:27.023
We will reach the correct formula soon.

00:18:27.023 --> 00:18:31.695
But for now, we simply hope that it is true.

00:18:31.695 --> 00:18:36.000
And we wonder: what then?

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.769
Let's assume it's true.

00:18:38.769 --> 00:18:42.922
Then it states the following:

00:18:42.922 --> 00:18:49.084
that the sum of the function f at different points k

00:18:49.084 --> 00:18:52.637
can be expressed through an integral,

00:18:52.637 --> 00:19:02.854
and the values of different derivatives of the function f, but at one point, as if this knowledge about the function at one point,

00:19:02.854 --> 00:19:09.075
can determine the sum over this function at different points k.

00:19:09.075 --> 00:19:12.000
As if.

00:19:13.644 --> 00:19:18.369
Let's rewrite it in another way.

00:19:20.525 --> 00:19:32.604
The Euler-Maclaurin formula talks about the difference between the sum (discrete integral) and the actual integral.

00:19:32.604 --> 00:19:41.806
This difference is determined by the values of the derivatives at one point "x".

00:19:41.806 --> 00:19:50.350
Oops.. there's a little mistake here. We integrate not up to 1, but up to x, of course.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:58.302
It's strange that there's no dependence on the values of derivatives at point 0.

00:19:58.302 --> 00:20:02.211
What is the difference between "0" and "x" in this formula?

00:20:02.211 --> 00:20:04.938
Seemingly nothing.

00:20:04.938 --> 00:20:07.184
And that's a good discovering.

00:20:07.184 --> 00:20:13.303
In fact, this formula is not correct and needs to be generalized. We'll understand how in a moment.

00:20:13.303 --> 00:20:21.432
Let me more  illustrate this difference.

00:20:21.432 --> 00:20:29.357
Let's take √x as the function f[x].

00:20:29.357 --> 00:20:38.775
Then this sum - it's the sum of these columns.

00:20:38.775 --> 00:20:46.106
And this integral - it's the area under the blue smooth curve.

00:20:46.106 --> 00:20:52.621
There's a subtle point: from which number do we sum to which.

00:20:52.621 --> 00:21:00.318
And accordingly, it turns out that the sum is either the columns above the curve,

00:21:00.318 --> 00:21:04.309
or the columns that fit inside the curve.

00:21:04.309 --> 00:21:07.074
Let's not delve into that for now,

00:21:07.074 --> 00:21:13.019
but it's as if this formula, it's a formula for the areas

00:21:13.019 --> 00:21:14.727
of these triangles.

00:21:14.727 --> 00:21:19.686
Either yellow triangles or gray triangles.

00:21:22.134 --> 00:21:27.260
Curvilinear triangles. They have one curved side.

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:32.480
By the way, it's clear that the sum of yellow and gray triangles is a simple thing.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:36.708
Because we can just shift these squares entirely to the left,

00:21:36.708 --> 00:21:41.044
and then the sum of all triangles, both gray and yellow,

00:21:41.044 --> 00:21:49.400
is simply: 1 multiplied by the height of the column, which is f[x].

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:53.165
So, if you know the sum of the yellow ones, you know the sum of the gray ones.

00:21:53.165 --> 00:21:54.416
Alright.

00:21:56.243 --> 00:22:05.297
So, let's write down the formula (which we think is true, but actually it's not) twice,

00:22:05.297 --> 00:22:07.587
when "x" equals "a" and when "x" equals "b",

00:22:07.587 --> 00:22:11.231
then subtract one from the other and here's what we'll get.

00:22:12.473 --> 00:22:18.905
The sum from a+1 to b equals this difference.

00:22:18.905 --> 00:22:22.995
And this formula already looks more symmetric.

00:22:22.995 --> 00:22:29.815
The points a and b are symmetrically included in this formula.

00:22:29.815 --> 00:22:41.107
And now it's clear that the sum of the areas of the triangles depends on the derivatives at point "a" and the derivatives at point "b".

00:22:41.107 --> 00:22:43.653
Okay.

00:22:45.114 --> 00:22:50.354
If we look at Wikipedia, we'll find the following formula:

00:22:50.354 --> 00:22:53.352
"S" is the sum, "I" is the integral.

00:22:53.352 --> 00:23:01.607
And instead of Zeta[-k], there are Bernoulli numbers.

00:23:01.607 --> 00:23:07.587
The sum there is not to infinity, but to "p", and that's correct. Infinity here is a mistake.

00:23:07.587 --> 00:23:11.292
And there's a certain remainder term here.

00:23:11.292 --> 00:23:21.692
Here I want to show that Zeta[-k] is simply Bernoulli numbers divided by k+1 with a minus sign.

00:23:21.692 --> 00:23:26.321
Furthermore, if you look into the "Euler-Maclaurin formula" section on Wikipedia,

00:23:26.321 --> 00:23:29.744
you'll find another formula.

00:23:29.744 --> 00:23:35.724
Here, the summation over "k", where "k" is multiplied by 2 inside the formula.

00:23:35.724 --> 00:23:40.231
Not surprisingly, because there's Zeta[-k] there.

00:23:40.231 --> 00:23:47.827
You see, those are numbers that are equal to zero. Every other number is equal to zero, except the first one.

00:23:47.827 --> 00:23:51.465
And the first term is taken out of the sum,

00:23:51.465 --> 00:24:02.340
and then, roughly speaking, you can only take the odd values of "k" in this formula.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:05.228
I actually prefer this notation of mine,

00:24:05.228 --> 00:24:11.538
where, on the contrary, this term is inside the sum.

00:24:11.538 --> 00:24:17.565
Moreover, I really want to put this term inside the formula.

00:24:17.565 --> 00:24:22.263
And I'll do it now.

00:24:22.263 --> 00:24:27.506
This "F" is the antiderivative of "f".

00:24:27.506 --> 00:24:30.161
What is an antiderivative?

00:24:30.161 --> 00:24:36.360
It's the k-th derivative, where k is equal to -1.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:40.549
Let's write the summation from k = -1

00:24:40.549 --> 00:24:43.133
and remove this term.

00:24:43.133 --> 00:24:48.986
This is another trick of the advanced math.

00:24:48.986 --> 00:24:53.395
What will happen when we do this?

00:24:53.395 --> 00:24:56.416
Here's what we'll get:

00:24:56.416 --> 00:25:08.971
we want Zeta[-k] (where k = -1), divided by k!, to be equal to -1.

00:25:08.971 --> 00:25:13.058
Here awaits us an unpleasantness.

00:25:13.058 --> 00:25:19.972
Zeta[1] and (-1)! are equal to infinity.

00:25:19.972 --> 00:25:26.175
But what's interesting is that if we let "s" approach -1 for this ratio,

00:25:26.175 --> 00:25:36.247
we'll get a limit equal to -1.

00:25:36.247 --> 00:25:39.733
We need exactly -1, not 1.

00:25:39.733 --> 00:25:45.859
Because there's a minus sign in front of the sum here.

00:25:45.859 --> 00:25:53.336
And this limit gives us a right to write this formula

00:25:53.336 --> 00:25:55.005
with this caveat,

00:25:55.005 --> 00:26:03.907
that for k = -1, this ratio should be considered equal to -1.

00:26:03.907 --> 00:26:10.767
So, as some joke, it turned into the Euler-Maclaurin formula.

00:26:10.767 --> 00:26:19.294
And all of this can be turned into a rigorous proof using phrases such as

00:26:19.294 --> 00:26:23.267
the sum and the integral are linear operators,

00:26:23.267 --> 00:26:26.689
the summation signs can be interchanged,

00:26:26.689 --> 00:26:36.504
and that transforming Zeta[-k] into z^k was only temporary in our reasoning and can be avoided.

00:26:36.504 --> 00:26:43.944
Here, I wrote these thoughts as equalities.

00:26:46.611 --> 00:26:48.205
Moving on.

00:26:48.205 --> 00:26:50.992
This formula,

00:26:50.992 --> 00:26:54.000
where the sum goes to infinity,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.546
actually doesn't work.

00:26:56.546 --> 00:27:03.635
It doesn't work even if you replace infinity with some finite value for the case

00:27:03.635 --> 00:27:15.754
where the function f has some peculiarity, an unpleasantness, at point "a".

00:27:15.754 --> 00:27:23.307
To avoid this unpleasantness at point "a" equal to zero for some functions f[t],

00:27:23.307 --> 00:27:25.855
we will apply the following trick.

00:27:25.855 --> 00:27:29.634
Here we have a graph of the function 1/√t,

00:27:29.634 --> 00:27:34.849
or more precisely, not the function itself, but these steps.

00:27:34.849 --> 00:27:42.649
And the sum of the reciprocals of the first n natural numbers is the blue area.

00:27:42.649 --> 00:27:50.890
So, the blue area can be calculated as the entire area under all the bars minus the yellow area.

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:59.831
This simple idea allows us to write the formula even for the case when s is equal to -1/2,

00:27:59.831 --> 00:28:04.211
that is, when you sum the reciprocals of square roots.

00:28:04.211 --> 00:28:06.540
And that's the whole idea.

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:14.090
But let's live in the world of the problem of summing the reciprocals of cubed natural numbers for now.

00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:17.293
It will be easier for us to start with.

00:28:17.293 --> 00:28:20.225
So, let's dwell in this problem for now.

00:28:20.225 --> 00:28:29.622
When we sum the reciprocals of cubed numbers, the area turns out to be finite. The total area.

00:28:29.622 --> 00:28:40.968
But when we choose 1/√n, the green area and the area under all the bars become infinite.

00:28:40.968 --> 00:28:47.237
Those who know calculus know this fact.

00:28:47.237 --> 00:28:50.979
Let's not think about this unpleasantness for now.

00:28:50.979 --> 00:28:59.299
Let's write that the blue area is the blue plus yellow minus yellow.

00:28:59.299 --> 00:29:02.555
And another thing we'll understand...

00:29:02.555 --> 00:29:09.699
We'll understand that the antiderivative for 1/(t^3) is this thing.

00:29:09.699 --> 00:29:16.877
And using the antiderivative, we can calculate the entire area under the smooth curve.

00:29:16.877 --> 00:29:24.646
And we can also calculate the area of all the triangles,

00:29:24.646 --> 00:29:26.972
these ones here,

00:29:26.972 --> 00:29:32.586
as the addition to the green area.

00:29:32.586 --> 00:29:38.821
We know the green area when the function f[t] is 1/(t^3).

00:29:38.821 --> 00:29:45.494
But how do we calculate the sum of the areas of all the triangles, both blue and yellow?

00:29:45.494 --> 00:29:47.674
The answer is as follows:

00:29:47.674 --> 00:29:50.220
like this.

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:52.181
What is a triangle?

00:29:52.181 --> 00:30:06.935
It's the area of a bar minus the green area within that bar.

00:30:06.935 --> 00:30:14.668
It is computed as the difference between the antiderivatives at the points t+1 and t.

00:30:14.668 --> 00:30:20.714
This is the area of the triangles for other values of s.

00:30:20.714 --> 00:30:23.467
Let's define it.

00:30:23.467 --> 00:30:28.497
The entire green area under the smooth curve for s = 3.

00:30:28.497 --> 00:30:32.141
It's written in another way here.

00:30:32.141 --> 00:30:37.891
Next, I finally want to define the function Zeta[s].

00:30:37.891 --> 00:30:41.825
It is the sum of the reciprocals of the s-th powers.

00:30:41.825 --> 00:30:49.083
Let's define the function Zeta[s] in this way when "s" is greater than one,

00:30:49.083 --> 00:30:54.371
then the sum converges.

00:30:54.371 --> 00:31:00.340
And then the area of all the triangles, Mathematica knows,

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:09.281
is equal to the area of all the bars minus the area under the smooth curve.

00:31:09.281 --> 00:31:15.188
I still want to write the areas of the triangles and work with the areas of the triangles.

00:31:15.188 --> 00:31:17.442
So, what do we do...

00:31:17.442 --> 00:31:24.995
We define the function Zeta[s] in this way, where "s" is greater than one.

00:31:24.995 --> 00:31:34.109
And we discover these moments,

00:31:34.109 --> 00:31:37.378
that

00:31:37.378 --> 00:31:46.572
the sum of the triangles is the sum of the bars minus the area under the smooth curve.

00:31:46.572 --> 00:31:51.639
Here again, the sum of the bars minus the area under the smooth curve.

00:31:51.639 --> 00:31:56.890
This is what the area of all the triangles is equal to.

00:31:59.337 --> 00:32:04.588
Zeta[4] - the sum of the reciprocals of the fourth powers, Mathematica knows.

00:32:04.588 --> 00:32:08.157
The sum of the fifth powers is unknown to Mathematica.

00:32:08.157 --> 00:32:13.044
In general, here's what we have, what Mathematica knows.

00:32:13.044 --> 00:32:18.813
Starting from the second power, it says there are numbers.

00:32:18.813 --> 00:32:25.270
And for even cases, it even knows the sum of the bars.

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:29.577
For the case when we take the hyperbola,

00:32:29.577 --> 00:32:33.396
the sum of the bars is equal to this sum.

00:32:33.396 --> 00:32:38.243
And Mathematica writes that it diverges.

00:32:39.923 --> 00:32:44.411
So... now it will become clear what I want to do.

00:32:44.411 --> 00:32:46.482
Here is this moment!

00:32:46.482 --> 00:32:48.297
What I want to do.

00:32:48.297 --> 00:32:51.829
I want to write down this formula:

00:32:51.829 --> 00:33:01.206
Zeta[s], the sum of the bars, is equal to the sum of the triangles plus the area under the smooth curve.

00:33:02.265 --> 00:33:04.659
Is idea clear?

00:33:04.659 --> 00:33:09.104
Here's what I couldn't tell you for so long.

00:33:09.104 --> 00:33:12.965
I want to write it like this.

00:33:12.965 --> 00:33:16.022
And here's the catch:

00:33:16.022 --> 00:33:21.858
if I take this sum (the sum of reciprocals),

00:33:21.858 --> 00:33:25.938
Mathematica says: I don't understand.

00:33:27.618 --> 00:33:32.876
Although it says this sum is the Zeta function,

00:33:32.876 --> 00:33:39.623
for s = 1/2, it says: the sum diverges.

00:33:39.623 --> 00:33:46.440
But if we take Zeta2[1/2]...

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:51.762
So... we need to define it.. I'm forgetting to do that..

00:33:51.762 --> 00:33:54.417
What does it say?

00:33:54.417 --> 00:33:58.716
It says there is an answer.

00:33:58.716 --> 00:34:01.885
It's not quite clear what's happening here.

00:34:01.885 --> 00:34:09.472
It simply says that this sum converges.

00:34:09.472 --> 00:34:17.622
The sum of areas of triangles is a converging thing.

00:34:17.622 --> 00:34:25.976
And this formula, which gave valid answers for s &gt; 1,

00:34:25.976 --> 00:34:31.481
we simply extended it for the case of s = 1/2.

00:34:31.481 --> 00:34:40.023
So, I showed you one formula that was true, obvious, and proven for s &gt; 1,

00:34:40.023 --> 00:34:47.464
and then I allowed myself to insert s = 1/2 into it

00:34:47.464 --> 00:34:55.088
and say: before, this was equal to Zeta[s],

00:34:55.088 --> 00:34:59.389
so let this thing be called Zeta[s] now.

00:34:59.389 --> 00:35:04.346
Let it be a series and diverge,

00:35:04.346 --> 00:35:08.134
but before, this formula gave the value of this series,

00:35:08.134 --> 00:35:19.886
so let this thing be a generalization of this analytic function of "s" for the case when s = 1/2.

00:35:19.886 --> 00:35:21.801
Not just 1/2.

00:35:21.801 --> 00:35:30.155
This series (the sum of triangle areas) will converge for s = 1/3, 1/4.

00:35:30.155 --> 00:35:39.569
For different numbers from 0 (greater than 0) to 1. Not just for s &gt; 1.

00:35:39.569 --> 00:35:41.676
That's how it is.

00:35:41.676 --> 00:35:49.117
Zeta2[3] gives the expected answer, Zeta[3].

00:35:49.117 --> 00:35:55.425
And for s = 1/2, it gives some new value,

00:35:55.425 --> 00:36:04.257
which Mathematica knows and it can do it too, and it calls it Zeta[1/2].

00:36:04.257 --> 00:36:16.741
To be continued...

